export const A1={title:'The nursing assistant: roles, rights and responsibilities',hook:'Nursing assistants give most of the hands-on care in nursing homes and much of it in hospitals: bathing, feeding, moving, measuring and noticing. Because they spend the most time with residents, they are often the first to see a change—and they protect residents’ rights every shift.',objectives:['Describe the Nursing Assisting event, test plan and Round Two skills.','Explain the nursing assistant’s role, training requirements and place on the care team.','Describe residents’ rights and legal and ethical responsibilities.','Recognize and report abuse and neglect.'],sections:[
['The Nursing Assisting event',[
'Round One is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** with no time announcements at ILC. The test plan: **nursing assistant roles and responsibilities 6%**, **anatomy and physiology 20%**, **infection control and safety 14%**, **communication and culture 10%**, **providing and reporting care 30%** and **disabilities and disorders 20%**. Questions come from Dugan’s *Nursing Assisting: A Foundation in Caregiving* and Roe’s *The Nursing Assistant: Essentials of Holistic Care*. Round Two uses a written scenario with one or more skills: **I** handwashing (3 minutes), **II** donning and doffing a full set of PPE (4), **III** making an unoccupied bed (8), **IV** making an occupied bed (10), **V** admitting a patient (8), **VI** transferring a patient with a specified disability from bed to chair or wheelchair (4), **VII** measuring and recording vital signs—TPR and aneroid blood pressure (10) and **VIII** ostomy care (8).',
'Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils, a **manual watch with a second hand** (no smartwatches) and barrier devices (non-latex gloves, gown, goggles or safety glasses and mask); HOSA provides a gait belt, or competitors may bring their own. A combined Round Two score of **70% or higher** is required for recognition; final rank adds test and skill scores, and ties go to the higher test score.',
match('Match the skill to its time limit',[['Handwashing','3 minutes'],['Full set of PPE','4 minutes'],['Unoccupied bed','8 minutes'],['Occupied bed','10 minutes'],['Transfer to chair or wheelchair','4 minutes'],['Vital signs (TPR and BP)','10 minutes']])]],
['The role and training',[
'Nursing assistants (also called certified nursing assistants, nurse aides or patient care assistants) work in **long-term care**, **hospitals**, **assisted living**, **rehabilitation** and **home care**. They provide **personal care** and activities of daily living (ADLs)—bathing, grooming, dressing, toileting, eating and mobility—measure vital signs, intake and output and weight, make beds, assist with positioning and transfers, observe and report changes and document care. They work under the supervision of a **licensed nurse** (RN or LPN/LVN) who assigns and **delegates** tasks. Federal rules from **OBRA 1987** require nurse aides in Medicare- and Medicaid-certified nursing homes to complete a training program of **at least 75 hours**, including **at least 16 hours of supervised practical training**, pass a **competency evaluation**, be listed on the **state nurse aide registry** and complete at least **12 hours of in-service education** each year. Many states require more hours.',
sort('Within the nursing assistant’s role?',['Within the role','Outside the role'],[['Measuring and recording vital signs',0],['Helping a resident eat lunch',0],['Changing an ostomy pouch when assigned',0],['Giving medications',1],['Inserting an IV',1],['Reporting a new reddened area on the heel',0],['Diagnosing an infection',1],['Making an occupied bed',0]])]],
['The care team and delegation',[
'The care team includes **physicians**, **nurse practitioners and physician assistants**, **registered nurses** (assessment, care planning, delegation), **licensed practical/vocational nurses**, **therapists** (physical, occupational, speech, respiratory), **dietitians**, **social workers**, **activity staff** and the resident and family. The nurse develops the **care plan**, and nursing assistants follow it. The **five rights of delegation**—right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction and communication, right supervision and evaluation—guide which tasks a nurse may assign. A nursing assistant should **refuse** a task that is outside their scope, that they have not been trained for, or that could harm a resident, explaining why and offering to help in another way.',
chk('A nurse asks you to give a resident her medications because the unit is busy. What should you do?',['Politely explain that giving medications is outside your scope and offer other help','Give the medications to help the nurse','Ask another nursing assistant to do it','Leave the medications at the bedside'],'Nursing assistants do not administer medications.')]],
['Residents’ rights',[
'**OBRA** established **residents’ rights** in nursing homes. Residents have the right to be treated with **dignity and respect**; to **privacy** and **confidentiality** of personal and medical information; to **make choices** about daily routines, activities and care; to be **informed** about their condition and treatment and to **participate in care planning**; to **refuse** treatment; to **manage their own finances** or choose who does; to keep and use **personal possessions**; to **voice grievances** without fear of retaliation; to **visits** and communication; and to be **free from abuse, neglect and restraints** used for discipline or convenience. Nursing assistants protect these rights in everyday actions: knocking and waiting before entering, closing curtains, explaining care, offering choices, handling belongings with care and keeping information private under **HIPAA**.',
match('Match the action to the right it protects',[['Knocking before entering','Privacy'],['Letting a resident choose her clothing','Making choices'],['Not discussing a diagnosis in the hallway','Confidentiality'],['Respecting a refusal and reporting it','Refusing treatment'],['Labeling and storing a resident’s photographs carefully','Personal possessions'],['Helping a resident file a complaint','Voicing grievances']])]],
['Legal issues, ethics and abuse',[
'Legal terms include **negligence** (failure to give expected care, causing harm), **malpractice**, **assault** (threatening), **battery** (touching without consent), **false imprisonment** (unlawful restraint), **invasion of privacy**, **defamation** and **fraud**. Ethical care means honesty, respect and putting residents’ needs first; nursing assistants never accept tips or gifts, and they avoid personal relationships with residents. **Abuse** includes **physical**, **sexual**, **verbal**, **emotional (psychological)** and **financial** abuse and **involuntary seclusion**; **neglect** is failure to provide needed care. Signs include unexplained injuries, fear, withdrawal, poor hygiene, weight loss and missing belongings. Nursing assistants **report suspected abuse immediately** to the nurse, following facility policy and state law, and document objective facts.',
kase('Seeing something wrong',{a:{text:'You see a coworker roughly pull a resident by the arm and shout at her for being slow. What should you do?',choices:[['Ensure the resident is safe and report what you saw to the nurse immediately','b','Correct. Abuse must be reported right away.',true],['Ignore it because the coworker is a friend','b','Failure to report abuse can itself be a violation.',false]]},b:{text:'How should you document what you saw?',choices:[['Objectively—what you saw and heard, the time and who was involved','c','Correct. Facts, not opinions.',true],['Write that the coworker is a bad person','c','Avoid opinions; record facts.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse investigates following facility policy, and the resident is checked for injury and comforted.',end:true}})]],
["Care settings, shift report and time management",["Nursing assistants work in several settings. In **long-term care (skilled nursing)**, residents may live for months or years, and care focuses on daily living, restorative care and quality of life. **Hospitals (acute care)** have shorter stays and sicker patients with frequent changes. **Assisted living** supports more independent residents, **rehabilitation units** focus on recovery, and **home care** brings care to the client’s residence. Each shift usually begins with a **report** from the nurse or previous shift: the assignment, residents’ conditions, special needs, isolation precautions and anything to watch for.","Good **time management** means planning the shift: review the assignment and care plans, note scheduled tasks (vital signs, meals, appointments), **prioritize** urgent needs—call lights, toileting, safety and changes in condition—before routine tasks, gather supplies before entering a room to save steps, and help teammates when your work is done. Nursing assistants also care for themselves: good body mechanics, healthy sleep and nutrition, and support from coworkers and supervisors reduce stress and **burnout**.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"What should be done first?","buckets":["High priority—act now","Routine—schedule into the shift"],"items":[["A resident’s call light for the bathroom",0],["A resident who has fallen",0],["Restocking the linen cart",1],["A resident reporting chest pain",0],["Changing a resident’s bed during the afternoon",1],["Charting a completed bath",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Test plan: providing and reporting care 30%, A&P 20%, disabilities and disorders 20%, infection control and safety 14%, communication 10%, roles 6%; eight skills need 70% combined.','OBRA: at least 75 training hours (16 supervised practical), competency evaluation, state registry, 12 in-service hours a year; nursing assistants work under a licensed nurse and do not give medications.','Protect residents’ rights (dignity, privacy, choices, refusal, possessions, grievances, freedom from abuse and restraints) and report suspected abuse immediately.'],terms:[['Activities of daily living','Basic self-care tasks such as bathing, dressing, eating and toileting.'],['Delegation','A nurse assigning a task to a qualified person.'],['OBRA','Federal law that set nurse aide training and residents’ rights.']],mcqs:[q('Which test area has the largest weight?','Providing and reporting care (30%)','Roles and responsibilities (6%)','Communication (10%)','Infection control (14%)','A&P and disorders are 20% each.'),q('OBRA requires nurse aide training of at least:','75 hours including 16 supervised practical hours','10 hours','500 hours','A bachelor’s degree','Plus a competency evaluation.'),q('Nursing assistants work under the supervision of:','A licensed nurse','A dietitian','The resident’s family','Another nursing assistant','The nurse delegates tasks.'),q('Which task is outside a nursing assistant’s scope?','Giving medications','Measuring vital signs','Making beds','Assisting with feeding','Report medication needs to the nurse.'),q('Knocking before entering protects the right to:','Privacy','Finances','Visitors','Grievances','Wait for permission.'),q('Restraining a resident without an order is:','False imprisonment','Libel','Delegation','Advocacy','Residents have the right to be free from restraints.'),q('Suspected abuse should be:','Reported to the nurse immediately','Discussed at lunch','Ignored without proof','Reported next week','Follow facility policy and state law.'),q('Annual in-service education required by OBRA for nurse aides is at least:','12 hours','1 hour','100 hours','None','Ongoing learning keeps skills current.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the nursing assistant’s role on the care team, the training required, and how nursing assistants protect residents’ rights and report abuse.',rubric:[['Role and duties',['adl','bathing','vital','observe','report','care plan']],['Training and supervision',['75','16','competency','registry','nurse','delegat']],['Residents’ rights',['dignity','privacy','choice','refuse','possessions','restraint']],['Abuse reporting',['abuse','neglect','report','immediately','objective','document']]],model:'Nursing assistants provide hands-on personal care and help residents with activities of daily living such as bathing, grooming, dressing, toileting, eating and mobility; they measure vital signs, intake and output and weight, make beds, position and transfer residents, observe and report changes and document care, following the care plan under the supervision of a licensed nurse who delegates tasks. OBRA requires at least 75 hours of training with at least 16 hours of supervised practical training, a competency evaluation, listing on the state registry and at least 12 hours of in-service education each year. Nursing assistants protect residents’ rights to dignity, privacy, confidentiality, choices, information and participation in care, refusing treatment, keeping personal possessions, voicing grievances and freedom from abuse, neglect and restraints—by knocking before entering, closing curtains, explaining care, offering choices and keeping information private. If they suspect physical, verbal, emotional, sexual or financial abuse or neglect, they make sure the resident is safe, report it to the nurse immediately and document objective facts.'},sources:[RULES,ECFR_NA,CMS_RIGHTS,BLS_NA]};

export const A2={title:'Communication, culture and reporting',hook:'Active listening means giving the patient your full attention while they are speaking—not assuming you understand or rejecting their complaint. Communication and culture are 10% of the test, and accurate reporting runs through every skill.',objectives:['Use active listening and therapeutic communication.','Provide culturally responsive care.','Communicate with residents who have sensory, speech or cognitive impairments.','Observe, report and document care accurately.'],sections:[
['Active listening and therapeutic communication',[
'**Active listening** means **giving the patient your full attention** while they speak: face them, make eye contact as culturally appropriate, nod, avoid interrupting and then reflect or paraphrase what you heard. It does **not** mean assuming you understand, interpreting hidden meanings or dismissing a complaint. **Therapeutic communication** techniques include open-ended questions (“How are you feeling today?”), reflecting feelings (“You seem worried”), clarifying, using silence and summarizing. **Barriers** include giving advice, false reassurance (“Everything will be fine”), changing the subject, using jargon, asking “why” questions that sound judgmental, talking down to residents (elderspeak such as “honey”) and arguing. **Nonverbal** communication—tone, posture, facial expression and touch—must match your words.',
sort('Active listening or a barrier?',['Active listening','Barrier'],[['Giving full attention while the patient speaks',0],['Assuming you know what the patient means',1],['Paraphrasing what the patient said',0],['Telling a patient their complaint is unfounded',1],['Using silence to let the patient continue',0],['Calling an older adult “sweetie”',1]]),
chk('Which statement best describes active listening?',['Giving the patient your full attention while they are speaking','Assuming you understand the basis for the statement','Interpreting the meaning of the comment','Rejecting a complaint as unfounded'],'A Dugan sample question.')]],
['Culture and care',[
'**Culture** includes the values, beliefs, language, customs and practices shared by a group. Cultural differences affect **communication** (eye contact, personal space, touch), **family roles** in decisions, **modesty** and the gender of caregivers, **diet** (religious food laws, vegetarianism, fasting), **spiritual practices** (prayer times, religious items), **beliefs about illness and pain** and **end-of-life customs**. **Culturally responsive care** begins with self-awareness and respect: ask about preferences rather than assume, use the resident’s preferred name, honor dietary and religious practices when possible, provide same-gender caregivers when requested and use qualified **interpreters**. Avoid **stereotypes**—every person is an individual, even within a culture.',
match('Match the situation to a culturally responsive action',[['A resident fasts during a religious observance','Report to the nurse and dietitian to plan meals and medications'],['A resident prefers a female caregiver for bathing','Honor the request when possible'],['A resident does not speak English','Use a qualified interpreter'],['A resident keeps a religious item on the bed','Handle it respectfully and do not remove it without permission'],['A family wants to be involved in decisions','Include them as the resident wishes'],['A resident avoids direct eye contact','Respect the norm and do not assume disinterest']])]],
['Communicating with impairments',[
'For **hearing loss**, face the resident in good light, reduce noise, speak clearly in a lower pitch without shouting, check that **hearing aids** are working and use writing or gestures. For **vision loss**, announce yourself, explain what you are doing, keep items in the same places and use the **clock method** for meals. For **aphasia** after a stroke, ask yes-or-no questions, allow time, use picture boards and do not finish sentences. For **dementia**, approach from the front, use the resident’s name, give **one-step directions**, offer simple choices and **redirect** instead of arguing. For **combative or angry** residents, stay calm, keep a safe distance, do not argue, remove triggers if possible and get help.',
flip('Communication adaptations',[['Hearing loss','Face the resident; check the hearing aid; lower pitch'],['Vision loss','Announce yourself; clock method at meals'],['Aphasia','Yes-or-no questions; picture boards; extra time'],['Dementia','One-step directions; redirect; calm tone'],['Anger','Stay calm; listen; keep distance; get help'],['Language barrier','Qualified interpreter; simple words; gestures']])]],
['Observing, reporting and documenting',[
'Nursing assistants use their senses to observe: **sight** (skin color, swelling, rashes, facial expression), **hearing** (breathing sounds, coughing, complaints), **smell** (odor of urine, wounds, breath) and **touch** (skin temperature, moisture, pulse). **Objective** data are measurable or observable facts (“temperature 100.6°F”); **subjective** data are what the resident reports (“I feel sick”), recorded in quotation marks. Report **immediately**: chest pain, trouble breathing, signs of stroke, changes in consciousness, falls, bleeding, vital signs outside the ordered ranges, new pain, sudden confusion and suspected abuse. Use a clear report such as **SBAR**—situation, background, assessment (what you observed) and request. **Documentation** is accurate, timely, objective and legible, in ink or the electronic record, never done before care is given, with errors corrected per policy. Many facilities use the **24-hour clock** (3:15 p.m. = 1515). HOSA skills end with documenting and reporting concerns to the nurse (judge).',
sort('Report immediately or document routinely?',['Report to the nurse immediately','Document routinely'],[['Resident says, “My chest feels tight”',0],['Sudden drooping of one side of the face',0],['Ate 75% of breakfast',1],['Resident fell in the bathroom',0],['Bed made and linens changed',1],['Blood pressure 190/110',0]])]],
['Applying communication skills',[
kase('A resident who refuses',{a:{text:'A resident says, “I don’t want a shower today—just leave me alone.” What is the best response?',choices:[['“You don’t feel like a shower today. Can you tell me more?”','b','Correct. Reflecting feelings and an open question invite communication.',true],['“You have to—it’s on the schedule.”','b','Forcing care violates the right to refuse.',false]]},b:{text:'She explains that the shower room is cold and she is embarrassed. What do you do?',choices:[['Offer a warm, private bed bath or another time, then report and document','c','Correct. Address the reason and respect her choice.',true],['Ignore her feelings and proceed','c','Residents’ preferences matter.',false]]},c:{text:'She agrees to a bed bath with the room warmed and her privacy protected. You document her preference for the care plan.',end:true}})]],
["Families, conflict and confidentiality",["Families are important members of the care team and often know the resident best. Welcome them, include them as the resident wishes, listen to their concerns and refer questions about diagnoses, medications or care plans to the nurse. Families may feel guilt, grief or frustration when a loved one moves into a facility, so patience matters. When **conflict** arises—with a family member, resident or coworker—stay calm, listen to understand, acknowledge feelings, avoid blaming or arguing, look for a solution within policy and involve the nurse or supervisor when needed. Conflicts between coworkers are discussed privately, never in front of residents.","**Confidentiality** applies everywhere: do not discuss residents in hallways, elevators, cafeterias or at home; never share resident information or photos on **social media**, even without names; give information only to authorized people; and protect records and screens. On the phone, identify yourself and the unit, take messages accurately and refer requests for information to the nurse.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Maintains confidentiality?","buckets":["Yes","No"],"items":[["Referring a caller’s questions about a diagnosis to the nurse",0],["Posting a photo of a resident’s birthday party online",1],["Discussing residents in the elevator",1],["Closing the chart screen before leaving the station",0],["Sharing a resident’s condition with your friends",1],["Talking with a family member in a private area",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Active listening means giving full attention; use open questions, reflection and silence; avoid false reassurance, jargon and elderspeak.','Provide culturally responsive care by asking about preferences, honoring diet and religious practices and using interpreters.','Report urgent changes immediately (SBAR); document objective and subjective data accurately, never before care is given.'],terms:[['Active listening','Giving full attention and reflecting the speaker’s message.'],['Culturally responsive care','Care that respects a person’s values, beliefs and practices.'],['SBAR','Situation, Background, Assessment, Request reporting format.']],mcqs:[q('Which best describes active listening?','Giving the patient your full attention while they speak','Assuming you understand the basis of a statement','Interpreting the patient’s meaning','Rejecting a complaint as unfounded','A Dugan sample question.'),q('Which response is therapeutic?','“You seem worried. Tell me more.”','“Don’t worry about it.”','“Why would you think that?”','“Let’s talk about something else.”','Reflection invites sharing.'),q('Which is objective data?','Pulse 112','“I feel dizzy.”','“My stomach hurts.”','“I’m tired.”','Measured facts are objective.'),q('For a resident with vision loss, meals can be described using:','The clock method','Medical abbreviations','Color only','No description','“Potatoes at 3 o’clock.”'),q('Which should be reported immediately?','Sudden facial drooping','Ate all of lunch','Watched television','Slept through the night','Possible stroke—report now.'),q('In 24-hour time, 9:45 p.m. is:','2145','0945','1945','2245','Add 12 to p.m. hours.'),q('Which is an example of culturally responsive care?','Asking a resident about dietary practices','Assuming all members of a culture are the same','Removing religious items to clean','Ignoring a request for a same-gender caregiver','Ask and respect preferences.'),q('Calling an older resident “sweetie” is an example of:','Elderspeak, a communication barrier','Therapeutic touch','Active listening','Cultural competence','Use the resident’s preferred name.')],frq:{prompt:'A resident who recently immigrated, speaks limited English and has hearing loss is admitted to your unit. Describe how you would communicate, provide culturally responsive care and report and document your observations.',rubric:[['Communication adaptations',['interpreter','hearing aid','face','noise','gesture','clear']],['Active listening',['full attention','listen','reflect','open-ended']],['Cultural responsiveness',['preference','diet','religio','modesty','family','respect']],['Reporting and documentation',['objective','subjective','report','nurse','document','sbar']]],model:'I would arrange a qualified interpreter rather than relying on family, face the resident in good light, reduce background noise, check that any hearing aid is in and working and speak clearly in a lower pitch, using gestures, pictures and written words when helpful. I would give my full attention, use simple open-ended questions through the interpreter, reflect feelings and allow time to respond. To provide culturally responsive care, I would ask about preferred name, food and religious practices, modesty and whether a same-gender caregiver is preferred, include family as the resident wishes, handle religious items respectfully and avoid assumptions or stereotypes. I would observe skin, vital signs, appetite, mobility, mood and pain and record objective facts and the resident’s own words in quotation marks. I would report anything urgent to the nurse immediately using SBAR and document accurately and promptly, including the resident’s preferences so they can be added to the care plan.'},sources:[RULES,THINK_CULT,HIPAA]};

export const A3={title:'Infection control: handwashing and PPE (Skills I and II)',hook:'Handwashing is the single most important way to prevent the spread of infection—and Skill I scores it in 21 precise steps. Skill II adds a full set of PPE. Infection control and safety make up 14% of the test and appear in every skill as “standard precautions.”',objectives:['Explain the chain of infection and how healthcare-associated infections spread.','Apply standard and transmission-based precautions.','Perform handwashing following HOSA Skill I.','Don and doff a full set of PPE following HOSA Skill II.'],sections:[
['How infections spread',[
'The **chain of infection** has six links: an **infectious agent** (bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites), a **reservoir** where it lives, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission** (direct or indirect contact, droplet, airborne, vehicle or vector), a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Older adults, people with chronic illness, wounds or catheters and those with weakened immune systems are especially susceptible. **Healthcare-associated infections (HAIs)**—such as catheter-associated urinary tract infections, pneumonia and *C. difficile*—are acquired while receiving care, and many are preventable. **Medical asepsis** (clean technique) reduces the spread of microorganisms; **surgical asepsis** (sterile technique) eliminates them. Signs of infection to report include fever, chills, redness, swelling, warmth, drainage, cough, burning urination, confusion and fatigue.',
order('Arrange the chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Standard and transmission-based precautions',[
'**Standard precautions** are used with **every** resident: treat blood, body fluids (except sweat), non-intact skin and mucous membranes as potentially infectious; perform hand hygiene; wear **gloves** when touching these, and add a **gown**, **mask** and **eye protection** when splashes are likely; handle sharps safely; clean equipment between residents; and follow respiratory hygiene. **Transmission-based precautions** are added for specific infections: **contact** (gloves and gown—MRSA, *C. difficile*, scabies), **droplet** (a mask within close range—influenza, pertussis) and **airborne** (an N95 respirator and a negative-pressure room—tuberculosis, measles, chickenpox). Place soiled linen in bags without shaking it, hold it away from your uniform and keep clean and dirty items separate.',
sort('Which precaution is added?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['MRSA in a wound',0],['C. difficile',0],['Scabies',0],['Influenza',1],['Pertussis',1],['Tuberculosis',2]])]],
['Handwashing (Skill I)',[
'Use **soap and water** when hands are visibly soiled, after caring for residents with *C. difficile* and after using the restroom; otherwise alcohol-based hand rub is effective. In Skill I (3 minutes), the competitor **stands back from the sink** to avoid contaminating clothing, **removes rings** and pushes the watch or sleeves above the wrist using a **paper towel**, turns on the faucet with a paper towel, adjusts the water to **warm** and discards the towel. They **wet hands, wrists and the skin 1–2 inches above the wrists** with **fingertips pointing down**, remove hands from the stream and apply soap to make a thick lather. They rub **palms together in a circular, counterclockwise motion**; push the fingers of each hand between the fingers of the other and rub up and down; **interlace fingers** and rub side to side; **interlock bent fingers** with the backs against the opposite palm; clean **under the fingernails** by rubbing them against the other palm and around the tops of the nails; rub each **thumb** in the opposite palm; and rub the **fingertips** of each hand in the middle of the other palm—all in **circular, counterclockwise** motions—for a **minimum of 20 seconds**. They rinse with **fingers pointed downward**, **dry hands then wrists** with a clean paper towel without shaking hands or touching the dispenser, **turn off the faucet with a clean, dry paper towel** and **discard it without touching the container** (42 points; 70% is 29.4).',
order('Handwashing (HOSA Skill I)',['Stand back from the sink; remove rings and push up the watch','Turn on warm water with a paper towel and discard it','Wet hands and wrists, fingertips down','Lather; rub palms in a circular, counterclockwise motion','Clean between fingers, backs of fingers, nails, thumbs and fingertips','Scrub for at least 20 seconds','Rinse with fingers pointing down','Dry hands then wrists; turn off the faucet with a dry towel; discard'])]],
['Donning and doffing a full set of PPE (Skill II)',[
'In Skill II (4 minutes), after assembling supplies and performing hand hygiene, the competitor **opens the gown** by holding it in front and letting it unfold without shaking, faces the back opening and puts **arms through the sleeves**, **fastens the neck**, then reaches behind and pulls the gown so it **covers all clothing** and secures it at the **waist**. They pick up the **mask** by the top strings or elastic without touching the inside, **tie the top strings first, then the bottom** (or pull the elastic), **pinch the nose strip** to fit tightly and fit the mask **snugly below the chin**. They place **goggles** over the eyes or glasses so they fit snugly. If right-handed, they slide one glove onto the **left hand** first (reverse if left-handed), use the gloved hand to put on the second glove, interlace fingers to smooth folds, **check gloves for tears, holes, cracks or discolored spots** and pull the **glove cuffs over the gown sleeves**.',
'To remove PPE when the judge says the skill is complete, the competitor removes **gloves** first—touching only the outside of one glove at the palm, pulling it off, holding it in the gloved hand, sliding two fingers under the remaining glove at the wrist and turning it inside out over the first glove—and drops them in the proper container. They remove **goggles**, then unfasten the **gown** at the neck and waist and remove it by **rolling the dirty side in**, holding it away from the body, and dispose of it. Finally they untie and remove the **mask** without touching its front, dispose of it and perform **hand hygiene** (53 points; 70% is 37.1).',
order('Doffing order in Nursing Assisting Skill II',['Remove gloves, turning them inside out','Remove goggles','Unfasten and roll off the gown, dirty side in','Untie and remove the mask without touching the front','Perform hand hygiene'])]],
['Applying infection control',[
kase('Contact precautions',{a:{text:'A resident on contact precautions for C. difficile needs help to the bathroom. What PPE do you need?',choices:[['Gown and gloves','b','Correct. Contact precautions require gloves and a gown.',true],['No PPE because you will only be there briefly','b','Contact with the resident or environment requires PPE.',false]]},b:{text:'After removing your PPE, how should you clean your hands?',choices:[['Wash with soap and water','c','Correct. Alcohol rub does not reliably remove C. difficile spores.',true],['Use alcohol-based hand rub only','c','Soap and water are required for C. difficile.',false]]},c:{text:'You wash your hands thoroughly and clean the shared equipment with the facility’s approved disinfectant.',end:true}})]],
["Equipment, sharps and isolation rooms",["Clean shared equipment—blood pressure cuffs, stethoscopes, thermometers, wheelchairs, lifts—with the facility’s approved disinfectant between residents, following the product’s **contact time**. Clean up **blood and body fluid spills** promptly with gloves, absorbent material and disinfectant, following policy. Never **recap** used needles; dispose of sharps immediately in **puncture-resistant sharps containers**, and report any needlestick or splash right away so you can receive evaluation. Employers must offer the **hepatitis B vaccine** to workers with exposure risk, and staff stay current with recommended vaccines and stay home when sick.","For **isolation (transmission-based precautions)**, a sign on the door lists required PPE. Put on PPE before entering, take only needed supplies into the room, use dedicated equipment when possible, keep the door closed for airborne precautions, remove PPE at the doorway or in the anteroom as policy directs and perform hand hygiene on leaving. Explain the reason for isolation to the resident and visitors, and remember that isolation can be lonely—spend time talking with the resident while following precautions. **Multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs)** such as MRSA, VRE and *C. difficile* spread easily on hands and equipment, which is why these steps matter.",{"t":"check","q":"After caring for a resident on contact precautions, where should PPE usually be removed?","options":["At the doorway or anteroom before leaving, followed by hand hygiene","At the nurses’ station","In the hallway after walking to the next room","It should be worn to the next resident"],"why":"Remove PPE before leaving the room to contain microorganisms."}]]
],takeaways:['Break the chain of infection; HAIs such as CAUTI and C. difficile are often preventable.','Standard precautions for everyone; add contact (gown, gloves), droplet (mask) or airborne (N95) precautions.','Skill I: warm water, fingertips down, counterclockwise circles, at least 20 seconds, dry hands then wrists, dry towel for the faucet; Skill II: gown, mask (top ties first), goggles, gloves over cuffs; remove gloves, goggles, gown, mask, hand hygiene.'],terms:[['Healthcare-associated infection','Infection acquired while receiving health care.'],['Standard precautions','Infection-control practices used with every resident.'],['Medical asepsis','Practices that reduce the spread of microorganisms.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill I, hands are held with fingertips:','Pointing down','Pointing up','Level','It does not matter','Water flows off the fingertips.'),q('The minimum scrubbing time in Skill I is:','20 seconds','5 seconds','2 minutes','10 seconds','Lather all surfaces.'),q('In Skill I, the faucet is turned off with:','A clean, dry paper towel','Bare hands','The elbow only','The used towel from drying','Avoid recontaminating hands.'),q('Which PPE is put on first in Skill II?','Gown','Gloves','Mask','Goggles','Then mask, goggles and gloves.'),q('Which PPE is removed first in Skill II?','Gloves','Mask','Gown','Goggles','Gloves are most contaminated.'),q('Mask strings are tied:','Top strings first, then bottom','Bottom first','Only the bottom','Not at all','This is a Skill II step.'),q('Which infection requires airborne precautions?','Tuberculosis','MRSA','C. difficile','Influenza','Use an N95 respirator.'),q('Glove cuffs should be pulled:','Over the sleeves of the gown','Under the sleeves','To the elbow','They are not adjusted','This seals the wrist.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how standard precautions and hand hygiene prevent infection, and describe how you would don and doff a full set of PPE following HOSA Skill II.',rubric:[['Chain of infection and precautions',['chain','standard precautions','every','transmission','contact','droplet','airborne']],['Hand hygiene',['soap','20 seconds','fingertips down','alcohol','c. diff','before','after']],['Donning sequence',['gown','neck','waist','mask','top strings','nose','goggles','gloves','cuff']],['Doffing sequence',['gloves','inside out','goggles','gown','roll','mask','hand hygiene']]],model:'Infection spreads through a chain of agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry and susceptible host, and hand hygiene breaks the most common link. Standard precautions are used with every resident—hand hygiene, PPE for expected contact with blood or body fluids, safe handling of sharps and linens and cleaning equipment—and contact, droplet or airborne precautions are added for specific infections. I wash with soap and water for at least 20 seconds with fingertips down when hands are soiled or after caring for a resident with C. difficile, and otherwise use alcohol rub before and after care and glove use. To don PPE, I perform hand hygiene, let the gown unfold without shaking, put my arms through the sleeves, fasten the neck and secure the waist so all clothing is covered; pick up the mask by the strings, tie the top then bottom, pinch the nose strip and fit it below the chin; place goggles snugly; and put on gloves, checking for tears and pulling the cuffs over the gown sleeves. To doff, I remove the gloves by turning them inside out without touching my skin, remove the goggles, unfasten and roll off the gown dirty side in away from my body, untie and remove the mask without touching the front, dispose of everything properly and perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HH,CDC_PPE]};

export const A4={title:'Safety, body mechanics and transfers (Skill VI)',hook:'Falls are among the most common and serious injuries in nursing homes and hospitals, and nursing assistants are injured lifting and moving residents more than almost any other workers. Safe transfers protect both—and Skill VI asks you to transfer a resident with a specified disability using a gait belt in four minutes.',objectives:['Prevent falls and use restraint alternatives.','Respond to fires, choking and other emergencies.','Use proper body mechanics and safe patient handling.','Transfer a resident with a weak side from bed to chair following Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Preventing falls',[
'Risk factors for falls include previous falls, weakness, poor balance, dizziness, vision problems, confusion, medications that cause drowsiness or low blood pressure, urgency to use the bathroom and unsafe footwear. Prevention: keep the **call light within reach** and answer it promptly; keep the bed in the **lowest position** with **wheels locked**; provide **nonskid footwear**; keep pathways clear and well-lit; use night-lights; assist residents to the bathroom on a schedule; keep personal items and water within reach; help residents rise slowly; and use assistive devices correctly. If a resident starts to fall, **do not try to stop the fall**—widen your stance, pull the resident close and **ease them to the floor**, protecting the head; then stay with the resident, call for the nurse and do not move them until they are checked. Every fall is reported and documented.',
sort('Fall prevention measure or fall risk?',['Prevents falls','Increases fall risk'],[['Bed in the lowest position with wheels locked',0],['Call light within reach',0],['Socks without nonskid soles',1],['Clutter in the walkway',1],['Scheduled toileting',0],['Dim lighting at night with no night-light',1]])]],
['Restraints and alternatives',[
'A **restraint** is any physical or chemical device that restricts movement or normal access to the body and that the resident cannot easily remove—including some side rails, vests, belts and sedating medications used for convenience. Restraints can cause injuries, pressure injuries, incontinence, depression, agitation and even death, and residents have the right to be **free from restraints** used for discipline or staff convenience. **Restraint alternatives** include frequent rounding, bed and chair alarms, low beds and floor mats, diversion activities, regular toileting, comfortable seating, adequate lighting, removing hazards and involving family. When a restraint is ordered by a physician and used as a last resort, nursing assistants follow facility policy: apply it correctly, check the resident at least every **15 minutes**, release it at least every **2 hours** to reposition, offer fluids and toileting, provide range of motion and document.',
chk('Which is a restraint alternative?',['A bed alarm and frequent rounding','A tight vest restraint','Tucking sheets tightly so the resident cannot get up','Sedating medication for staff convenience'],'Alternatives meet the resident’s needs without restricting movement.')]],
['Emergencies',[
'In a **fire**, follow **RACE**: **R**escue residents in immediate danger, **A**ctivate the alarm, **C**ontain the fire by closing doors and windows and **E**xtinguish if safe or evacuate. Use an extinguisher with **PASS**: **P**ull the pin, **A**im at the base, **S**queeze the handle and **S**weep side to side. **Oxygen** increases fire risk: no smoking or open flames. For a conscious adult who is **choking** and cannot speak, cough or breathe, call for help and give **abdominal thrusts** (the Heimlich maneuver) until the object is expelled or the person becomes unresponsive, then begin CPR as trained. For **seizures**, protect the resident from injury, move objects away, do not restrain or place anything in the mouth, time the seizure and turn the resident on their side afterward. For **fainting**, lower the resident safely, elevate the legs if appropriate and call the nurse. Know the facility’s emergency codes and evacuation routes.',
flip('Emergency acronyms and actions',[['RACE','Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish or evacuate'],['PASS','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep'],['Choking adult','Abdominal thrusts until expelled or unresponsive'],['Seizure','Protect, do not restrain, nothing in the mouth, time it'],['Fainting','Lower safely, legs up, call the nurse'],['Oxygen in use','No smoking, candles or open flames']])]],
['Body mechanics and safe patient handling',[
'**Body mechanics** is the safe, efficient use of the body. Keep a **wide base of support** (feet about shoulder-width apart), **bend at the knees and hips**, keep the **back straight**, hold objects **close to your body**, use the strong leg and arm muscles, **avoid twisting**—pivot your feet instead—**push rather than pull** when possible and raise the bed to a comfortable working height (about hip level). **Safe patient handling** programs use mechanical lifts, slide sheets and transfer devices and encourage getting help rather than lifting alone. A **gait (transfer) belt** gives a secure handhold during transfers and walking; it is placed **over clothing**, around the waist, buckled in front and snug enough that only your **fingers fit under it**. It is not used over a new incision, abdominal tubes or certain medical conditions without the nurse’s approval.',
sort('Good body mechanics?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Bending at the knees with a straight back',0],['Twisting the waist while lifting',1],['Holding the load close to the body',0],['Feet together when moving a resident',1],['Raising the bed to hip level before care',0],['Lifting a heavy resident alone instead of getting help',1]])]],
['Transferring a resident with a weak side (Skill VI)',[
'In Skill VI (4 minutes), the competitor assembles equipment, performs hand hygiene, greets, identifies and explains to the resident and provides privacy. They place the **chair or wheelchair at the head of the bed facing the foot, or at the foot facing the head**, positioned so the resident can **use the strong side**; **lock the wheelchair wheels** and fold up the footrests. They **lower the bed and lock its wheels**, **elevate the head of the bed**, **lower the side rail on the exit side** and **fanfold the linens** to the foot. They help the resident **dangle the feet**, **observing for signs of stress** such as dizziness or pallor, and put on a robe and **non-slip slippers**. They apply the **gait belt** over clothing at the waist with the buckle in front, tight enough that fingers fit under it. Standing in front of the resident with **feet about 12 inches apart**, they **brace one or both knees against the resident’s knees** (especially the weak knee), instruct the resident to push against the bed on a signal such as a **count of three**, and at the signal help the resident stand, **lifting up on the belt**. The resident takes **small steps** toward the chair and **pivots** until the backs of the legs touch the seat, places hands on the armrests, bends at the knees and sits. The competitor adjusts the footrests, removes the gait belt, drapes the resident, checks comfort, performs hand hygiene and communicates throughout (37 points; 70% is 25.9).',
order('Bed-to-wheelchair transfer (HOSA Skill VI)',['Hand hygiene, identify, explain and provide privacy','Place the wheelchair on the resident’s strong side; lock wheels; lift footrests','Lower and lock the bed; raise the head; lower the exit-side rail','Help the resident dangle; watch for dizziness','Put on robe and non-slip slippers; apply the gait belt','Stand in front with feet 12 inches apart; brace the knees','On the count of three, lift on the belt as the resident pushes up','Pivot until the legs touch the seat; lower to sit; adjust footrests and remove the belt']),
kase('Dizziness during a transfer',{a:{text:'While dangling at the edge of the bed, a resident who had a stroke says he feels dizzy and looks pale. What should you do?',choices:[['Have him stay seated or lie back, stay with him and tell the nurse','b','Correct. Do not continue a transfer when a resident shows signs of stress.',true],['Hurry the transfer before he gets worse','b','Moving a dizzy resident risks a fall.',false]]},b:{text:'After a few minutes he feels better. His left side is weak. Where should the wheelchair be placed?',choices:[['On his right (strong) side','c','Correct. He pivots toward and uses his strong side.',true],['On his left (weak) side','c','Place the chair so he can use his strong side.',false]]},c:{text:'You complete the transfer safely with the gait belt and document his dizziness and tolerance.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Prevent falls: call light within reach, low locked bed, nonskid footwear, clear paths; ease a falling resident to the floor and report every fall.','Residents have the right to be free from restraints; use alternatives; RACE and PASS for fire; abdominal thrusts for choking.','Body mechanics: wide base, bend knees, straight back, load close, pivot don’t twist; Skill VI: chair on the strong side, wheels locked, dangle, gait belt with fingers under it, brace knees, count of three, pivot and sit.'],terms:[['Restraint','Device or drug that restricts movement and cannot be easily removed.'],['Gait belt','Belt worn around the waist to assist transfers and walking.'],['Body mechanics','Safe, efficient use of the body to move and lift.']],mcqs:[q('If a resident begins to fall, you should:','Ease the resident to the floor, protecting the head','Try to hold the resident up','Let go','Lift the resident back to bed alone','Do not fight the fall.'),q('In Skill VI, the wheelchair is placed so the resident can use:','The strong side','The weak side','Neither side','Only the arms','Pivot toward the strong side.'),q('The gait belt should be tight enough that:','Your fingers fit under it','Nothing fits under it','It slides freely','It is around the chest','It is placed over clothing.'),q('In Skill VI, the competitor stands with feet about:','12 inches apart','Together','3 feet apart','1 inch apart','A wide base of support.'),q('Before standing, the resident dangles to:','Check for dizziness or other signs of stress','Exercise the arms','Fall asleep','Warm up the bed','Prevent fainting during transfer.'),q('Which is a restraint alternative?','A bed alarm','A vest restraint','Sedation for convenience','Tucking sheets tightly','Alternatives preserve freedom.'),q('In PASS, the extinguisher is aimed at:','The base of the fire','The top of the flames','The smoke','The ceiling','Sweep side to side.'),q('Correct body mechanics include:','Pivoting the feet instead of twisting','Bending at the waist','Holding loads far away','Lifting with the back','Use the leg muscles.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how you would safely transfer a resident with left-sided weakness from bed to a wheelchair following HOSA Skill VI, including fall prevention and body mechanics.',rubric:[['Preparation',['identify','explain','privacy','strong side','right','lock','footrest']],['Bed and resident setup',['lower','lock','head of bed','side rail','dangle','dizz','slippers']],['Gait belt and technique',['gait belt','buckle','fingers','12 inches','knee','count of three','pivot']],['Body mechanics and safety',['wide base','bend knees','straight back','no twist','report','document']]],model:'After hand hygiene, I would greet and identify the resident, explain the transfer and provide privacy. Because his left side is weak, I would place the wheelchair on his right (strong) side at the head of the bed facing the foot, lock the wheels and fold up the footrests. I would lower and lock the bed, raise the head of the bed, lower the side rail on the exit side and fanfold the linens, then help him dangle his legs while watching for dizziness or pallor, and put on a robe and non-slip slippers. I would apply the gait belt over his clothing at the waist, buckle in front, snug enough that my fingers fit under it. Standing in front of him with my feet about 12 inches apart, knees bent and back straight, I would brace my knee against his weak left knee, tell him to push up from the bed on the count of three and lift up on the belt as he stands, then have him take small steps and pivot toward his strong side until his legs touch the seat, place his hands on the armrests and sit. I would adjust the footrests, remove the belt, cover him, check comfort and keep the call light in reach, then perform hand hygiene and document, reporting any dizziness to the nurse.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_STEADI,OSHA_SPH]};

export const A5={title:'Skin, muscles, bones and nerves: structure, disorders and care',hook:'What is the primary purpose of passive range-of-motion exercises? To prevent contractures and atrophy. The integumentary, musculoskeletal and nervous systems are where immobility does its damage—pressure injuries, frozen joints and falls—and where nursing assistants make a difference every shift.',objectives:['Describe the structure and function of the skin, muscles, bones and nervous system.','Prevent and report pressure injuries.','Perform range-of-motion exercises and care for residents with musculoskeletal disorders.','Care for residents with stroke, Parkinson’s disease, multiple sclerosis and spinal cord injury.'],sections:[
['The integumentary system and pressure injuries',[
'The skin is the body’s largest organ: it **protects** against infection and injury, **regulates temperature**, senses touch, pain and temperature, prevents fluid loss and helps make vitamin D. Its layers are the **epidermis**, **dermis** (blood vessels, nerves, glands, hair follicles) and **subcutaneous tissue** (fat). With aging, skin becomes thinner, drier and more fragile. **Pressure injuries** develop over **bony prominences**—sacrum, coccyx, hips, heels, elbows, shoulder blades, ears and back of the head—when pressure, often with **shear** and **friction**, reduces blood flow. Early signs are **redness that does not fade** (or color change in darker skin), warmth, swelling or tenderness. Prevention: **reposition at least every 2 hours** in bed (every hour in a chair, or as the care plan directs), keep skin clean and dry, manage incontinence promptly, keep linens smooth and wrinkle-free, use pillows and pressure-relieving devices, lift rather than drag, encourage nutrition and fluids and **never massage reddened areas**. Report any change immediately.',
label('skin','Layers of the skin',[['epidermis','Epidermis','Outer protective layer.'],['dermis','Dermis','Contains vessels, nerves, glands and follicles.'],['hypodermis','Subcutaneous tissue','Fat that cushions and insulates.'],['sweat','Sweat gland','Helps regulate temperature.'],['receptor','Sensory receptor','Detects touch and pressure.'],['vessel','Blood vessel','Pressure reduces flow and damages tissue.']])]],
['The musculoskeletal system',[
'The **206 bones** of the adult skeleton support and protect the body, store minerals and make blood cells in the **marrow**. **Joints** allow movement; **ligaments** connect bone to bone and **tendons** connect muscle to bone. **Muscles** move the body, maintain posture and produce heat. Disorders include **arthritis**—**osteoarthritis** (wear of joint cartilage) and **rheumatoid arthritis** (autoimmune inflammation)—**osteoporosis** (thin, brittle bones that fracture easily, especially in older women), **fractures**, **hip and knee replacements** and **amputations** (with possible **phantom pain**, which is real). After hip replacement, common precautions include **not crossing the legs**, **not bending the hip past 90°** and keeping the leg from turning inward, using an **abduction pillow** between the legs as ordered. Immobility causes **contractures** (permanent shortening of muscles and tendons) and **atrophy** (wasting of muscle).',
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['cartilage','Cartilage','Cushions bone ends; wears down in osteoarthritis.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Inflamed in rheumatoid arthritis.'],['ligament','Ligament','Connects bone to bone.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Encloses the joint.'],['bone','Bone','Thin and brittle in osteoporosis.']])]],
['Range of motion',[
'**Range-of-motion (ROM)** exercises move each joint through its full normal range. **Active ROM** is done by the resident, **active-assisted** with help and **passive ROM** by the nursing assistant for residents who cannot move. The **primary purpose of passive ROM is to prevent contractures and atrophy**; ROM also improves circulation and maintains joint mobility. Guidelines: follow the care plan, explain, provide privacy, use good body mechanics, **support the limb above and below the joint**, move each joint **slowly, gently and smoothly**, repeat each movement as ordered (often 3–5 times), **never force a joint** past the point of resistance or pain and **stop and report pain**. Movements include **flexion** (bending), **extension** (straightening), **abduction** (away from the midline), **adduction** (toward the midline), **rotation**, **pronation** (palm down), **supination** (palm up), **dorsiflexion** (toes up) and **plantar flexion** (toes down).',
match('Match the movement to its definition',[['Flexion','Bending a joint'],['Extension','Straightening a joint'],['Abduction','Moving away from the midline'],['Adduction','Moving toward the midline'],['Supination','Turning the palm up'],['Dorsiflexion','Pointing the toes up toward the shin']]),
chk('What is the primary purpose of passive range-of-motion exercises?',['Prevent contractures and atrophy','Prevent bone demineralization','Preserve muscle mass','Increase muscle tone'],'A Dugan sample question.')]],
['The nervous system and its disorders',[
'The nervous system has the **central nervous system** (brain and spinal cord) and the **peripheral nervous system** (nerves throughout the body). The **cerebrum** controls thinking, movement and sensation—each side controls the **opposite side of the body**—the **cerebellum** controls balance and coordination, and the **brainstem** controls breathing and heart rate. A **stroke (CVA)** interrupts blood flow to the brain; recognize it with **BE FAST** and report immediately. Effects may include **hemiplegia** (paralysis of one side), **aphasia**, **dysphagia** (trouble swallowing) and **one-sided neglect**. Care: **dress the weak side first and undress the strong side first**, place items on the strong side, follow swallowing precautions (upright, small bites, thickened liquids if ordered) and encourage independence. **Parkinson’s disease** causes tremors, rigidity, slow movement and a shuffling gait; **multiple sclerosis** damages nerve coverings, causing weakness, fatigue and vision problems; **spinal cord injuries** cause **paraplegia** (lower body) or **quadriplegia (tetraplegia)** (all four limbs), with risks of pressure injuries, bladder and bowel problems and autonomic dysreflexia.',
sort('Correct care for a resident with right-sided weakness after stroke?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Putting the shirt on the right arm first',0],['Taking the shirt off the left arm first',0],['Placing the call light on the left (strong) side',0],['Giving thin liquids despite a thickened-liquid order',1],['Doing every task for the resident to save time',1],['Offering small bites with the resident upright',0]])]],
['Sensory system',[
'**Vision** problems include **cataracts** (clouded lens), **glaucoma** (increased pressure that can damage the optic nerve) and **macular degeneration** (loss of central vision); clean eyeglasses, keep items in place and use the clock method. **Hearing** loss is common with aging; keep hearing aids clean with working batteries, store them safely when removed and never immerse them in water. Report ear drainage, sudden hearing or vision changes and eye pain. Sensory loss increases the risk of falls and isolation, so good lighting, clear communication and social contact matter.',
kase('A reddened heel',{a:{text:'While giving morning care, you notice a reddened area on a resident’s right heel that does not fade after you remove pressure. What do you do?',choices:[['Keep pressure off the heel, report to the nurse and document','b','Correct. Persistent redness may be an early pressure injury.',true],['Massage the heel to improve circulation','b','Massage can damage the tissue further.',false]]},b:{text:'How can you protect the heel going forward?',choices:[['Float the heels with a pillow under the calves and reposition on schedule','c','Correct. Offloading the heel relieves pressure.',true],['Place the heel directly on a folded towel','c','The heel still bears pressure.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse assesses the area and adds heel protection to the care plan.',end:true}})]],
["Ambulation and assistive devices",["Walking keeps residents strong, prevents complications of immobility and supports independence. When **ambulating** a resident, apply a **gait belt**, have the resident wear nonskid footwear, walk **slightly behind and to the weak side**, holding the belt, and watch for fatigue, dizziness or pain. **Canes** are held on the **strong side**; the resident moves the cane and the **weak leg** forward together, then the strong leg. **Walkers** are lifted or rolled forward a short distance, then the resident steps into the walker with the weak leg first, followed by the strong leg—residents should not pull on a walker to stand. **Crutches** require fitting and specific gaits taught by therapists. **Wheelchairs** must have the brakes locked during transfers and footrests positioned for safety.","Residents may also use **prosthetic** limbs, **orthotic** braces and **splints**. Apply them as instructed, check the skin underneath for redness or breakdown and report problems. Encourage residents to do as much as they can, and praise their efforts—rehabilitation depends on practice.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the device to correct use","pairs":[["Cane","Held on the strong side; moves with the weak leg"],["Walker","Moved forward first, then step in weak leg first"],["Gait belt","Held from behind and to the weak side while walking"],["Wheelchair","Brakes locked during transfers"],["Prosthesis","Check the skin beneath for redness"],["Splint","Applied as instructed to support a joint"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Reposition at least every 2 hours, keep skin clean and dry, never massage reddened areas and report redness over bony prominences.','Passive ROM prevents contractures and atrophy: support the joint, move slowly, never force, stop at pain; hip replacement—no crossing legs or bending past 90°.','Stroke affects the opposite side: dress the weak side first, follow swallowing precautions; recognize Parkinson’s, MS and spinal cord injury needs.'],terms:[['Contracture','Permanent shortening of a muscle or tendon causing a fixed joint.'],['Atrophy','Wasting or decrease in size of a muscle.'],['Hemiplegia','Paralysis of one side of the body.']],mcqs:[q('What is the primary purpose of passive range-of-motion exercises?','Prevent contractures and atrophy','Prevent bone demineralization','Preserve muscle mass','Increase muscle tone','A Dugan sample question.'),q('Pressure injuries most often develop over:','Bony prominences','The abdomen','The palms','The thighs only','Pressure reduces blood flow.'),q('A reddened area over the sacrum should be:','Kept free of pressure and reported','Massaged','Covered with lotion','Ignored','Never massage reddened skin.'),q('A resident with left-sided weakness should be dressed:','Left side first','Right side first','Both at once','Only on the right','Weak side in first, out last.'),q('Moving a limb away from the midline is:','Abduction','Adduction','Flexion','Extension','Adduction moves toward.'),q('After hip replacement, a common precaution is:','Do not cross the legs','Bend the hip past 90°','Sit on very low chairs','Turn the leg inward','Follow the care plan.'),q('Which disease causes tremors and a shuffling gait?','Parkinson’s disease','Osteoporosis','Cataracts','Diabetes','Fall risk increases.'),q('During passive ROM, if the resident reports pain you should:','Stop and report it','Push gently past the pain','Move faster','Continue as ordered','Never force a joint.')],frq:{prompt:'A resident who had a stroke has right-sided weakness and spends most of the day in bed. Describe how you would prevent pressure injuries and contractures and adapt daily care for the weakness.',rubric:[['Pressure injury prevention',['reposition','2 hours','bony','dry','massage','report','linens']],['ROM technique and purpose',['range of motion','passive','contracture','atrophy','support','force','pain']],['Stroke care adaptations',['weak side','right','dress','strong side','swallow','upright','independence']],['Observation and reporting',['redness','report','nurse','document','skin']]],model:'To prevent pressure injuries, I would reposition her at least every two hours or as the care plan directs, use pillows to keep bony areas apart and float her heels, keep her skin clean and dry and linens smooth, manage incontinence promptly, lift rather than drag her, encourage fluids and nutrition and never massage reddened areas, reporting any redness that does not fade. To prevent contractures and atrophy, I would perform passive range-of-motion exercises as ordered on her weak right side and encourage active ROM on her left—supporting each joint above and below, moving slowly and gently through flexion, extension, abduction, adduction and rotation, repeating each movement as ordered and stopping and reporting pain. For daily care, I would dress her right (weak) side first and undress her left (strong) side first, place her call light and belongings on her strong side, follow swallowing precautions with her sitting upright and taking small bites and encourage her to do as much as she can. I would observe her skin, mobility and mood, report changes to the nurse and document care.'},sources:[RULES,OS_SKIN,AHRQ_PU,ASA_STROKE]};
